        "title": "Who Was Here First?",
        "reading": "Long before any European ship crossed the Atlantic Ocean, millions of people already called the Americas home. Scientists called archaeologists study this deep past by digging up artifacts, which are objects people left behind. Stone tools, clay pots, and ancient fire pits all tell a story. Most scientists believe the first peoples arrived more than 15,000 years ago, walking from Asia when thick ice joined the continents. They followed herds of large animals and slowly spread across North and South America. Over thousands of years, these first peoples built villages, farmed corn and beans, and raised great mounds of earth. Native peoples are still here today, keeping their languages and traditions alive. Much later, in Florida, free Black people escaping slavery joined Native communities and built free towns together. These Black Seminoles show that the story of the first peoples connects to many other American stories. Archaeologists keep digging, and every year brings new discoveries about the first Americans. Their work reminds us that history is a puzzle we are still solving, one artifact at a time.",
