        "title": "A Day with the First Peoples",
        "reading": "Imagine waking up in a village of the first peoples. Your home might be a longhouse shared with many families, or a round hut made of wood and clay. After breakfast, the children head out with the adults. Some gather berries, nuts, and roots. Others check traps or fish with woven nets. Everyone has a job, and even young children help. They carry water, sort seeds, or watch the younger ones. Tools are made from stone, bone, and wood because there is no metal yet. In the evening, families gather around the fire. Elders tell stories that teach lessons and keep history alive, since nothing is written down. Food is shared so no one goes hungry. Life is hard work, but the village works together like one big family. Children learned by watching and doing, not from books. A boy might practice with a small bow while his father hunted deer. A girl might learn which berries were safe by gathering beside her mother. These lessons, passed down for thousands of years, kept every village strong.",
