        "title": "From Flowers to Seeds",
        "reading": "Flowers are more than pretty decorations. They are the plant's way of making seeds for the next generation. Inside a flower, powdery pollen must reach the flower's center. Bees, butterflies, and hummingbirds carry pollen as they sip sweet nectar, traveling from bloom to bloom. Wind can carry pollen too. Once pollen arrives, the flower can grow seeds. A sunflower head can hold a thousand seeds. Those seeds fall, blow away, or hitch rides on animal fur. Each seed is a tiny packed lunch with a baby plant inside, waiting for water and warmth. When spring comes, the seed sprouts, and the whole cycle begins again with roots, stems, leaves, and flowers. People help seeds travel too. Farmers plant them in neat rows, and gardeners tuck them into soft soil. Squirrels bury nuts and forget some, accidentally planting oak trees. Even mud on your shoes can carry seeds to new places. Every garden you plant joins nature's delivery service.",
