        "title": "The Aztec: An Empire on a Lake",
        "reading": "The Aztec built their capital, Tenochtitlan, on an island in the middle of a lake in Mexico. It was one of the largest cities in the world in the 1500s, bigger than London at the time. Causeways, or raised roads, connected the island to the shore. Canoes crowded the water, carrying food and goods to huge markets. At the city's heart stood the Great Temple, where priests honored their gods. The emperor ruled millions of people, and conquered towns paid tribute in corn, cloth, and feathers. But the Aztec faced a problem: how do you feed a giant city on a lake? Their answer was brilliant. They built floating gardens called chinampas, rich with mud and plants, that grew enormous harvests. Across the ocean, the Songhai Empire in West Africa also fed great cities through trade and farming, proving that clever farmers built empires on both sides of the Atlantic.",
