        "title": "How Plants Cook Their Food",
        "reading": "Plants do something no animal can do: they cook their own food using sunlight. This process is called photosynthesis, which means putting together with light. It happens inside leaves, in tiny green parts called chloroplasts. The recipe needs three ingredients. First, sunlight, caught by a green coloring called chlorophyll. Second, water, pulled up from the soil by the roots. Third, air, which holds a gas called carbon dioxide that enters through tiny leaf holes. The plant mixes these ingredients and cooks up sugar, its food. As a bonus, it releases oxygen into the air, the very air we breathe. Every breath you take connects you to a plant's kitchen. Photosynthesis is slow but powerful. A single large tree can make enough oxygen for two people for a whole year. Forests pull huge amounts of carbon from the air as they grow. That is why planting trees helps clean the atmosphere. Every leaf is a tiny factory working for the planet.",
