        "title": "Villages of the Eastern Woodlands",
        "reading": "East of the Mississippi River, forests stretched for hundreds of miles. The Native peoples who lived there are called Eastern Woodlands tribes. They built villages near rivers and lakes, where the soil was rich and fish were plenty. Many lived in longhouses, long wooden homes where several related families, called a clan, lived together under one roof. Each clan was often named for an animal, such as the Wolf Clan or the Turtle Clan. The Haudenosaunee, also called the Iroquois, joined five nations into a powerful league with a Great Law of Peace. Far to the south, in Florida, Africans escaping slavery found freedom among Native tribes there. They built free towns together and became known as the Black Seminoles, showing how Native and African peoples shaped each other's stories. The Haudenosaunee league inspired later thinkers. Some historians say its Great Law of Peace influenced ideas about democracy in America. Whether or not that is true, the league showed that different nations could unite under shared laws. It lasted for centuries, longer than many modern countries.",
