        "title": "Following the Buffalo",
        "reading": "On the Great Plains, the grass stretched to the horizon and huge buffalo herds darkened the land. The Plains tribes, like the Lakota, Cheyenne, and Comanche, built their lives around the buffalo. They were nomads, moving their villages to follow the herds. Their homes were tipis, cone-shaped tents of buffalo hide that could be taken down and packed onto a travois in minutes. Every part of the buffalo was used: meat for food, hides for clothing and tipis, bones for tools, and even the stomach as a cooking pot. Horses, brought by the Spanish, changed everything. A hunter on horseback could keep up with a running herd. Children learned to ride almost as soon as they could walk, and skilled riders became heroes of the tribe. The buffalo nearly vanished in the 1800s when hunters killed them by the millions. Fewer than a thousand remained. Today, protected herds have grown back to hundreds of thousands on preserves and ranches. The comeback shows that even great damage can be repaired when people act.",
