        "title": "Black Cowboys of the Plains",
        "reading": "After the Civil War, cattle ranching boomed on the Plains, and cowboys were needed to drive huge herds to market. About one in four cowboys was Black. These men were skilled riders, ropers, and trail cooks. Nat Love was one of the most famous. Born enslaved in Tennessee, he headed west at sixteen and became a legendary cowboy known as Deadwood Dick. He won a roping contest in Deadwood and could handle a lasso, a rifle, and a wild horse. Bose Ikard was another respected Black cowboy, so trusted that rancher Charles Goodnight had him carry large sums of money. Movies often show cowboys as only white, but the real West was far more mixed, and Black cowboys helped build the cattle industry. Cowboys developed their own culture: songs sung to calm herds at night, tall tales told around fires, and gear like chaps and wide hats made for the work. Rodeos grew from the contests cowboys held to show their skills. The cowboy way of life became a symbol of America itself.",
