        "title": "Farming the Desert",
        "reading": "The Southwest is hot and dry, but tribes like the Hopi, Zuni, and Pueblo peoples learned to farm there. They built pueblos, villages of connected adobe homes stacked like apartment buildings, some five stories tall. Adobe bricks of clay, sand, and straw kept homes cool by day and warm by night. Water was precious, so farmers became clever. They planted corn deep in the sand where moisture hid, and they built small dams to catch rare rains. They grew corn, beans, squash, and cotton in fields watered by hand. The Hopi dry-farmed, growing corn with almost no irrigation at all. Their success in such a harsh land shows deep knowledge passed down for hundreds of generations. Pueblo peoples still live in their ancient villages today. Taos Pueblo in New Mexico has been continuously inhabited for a thousand years. Its adobe buildings rise five stories, just as the reading describes. Visiting Taos is like stepping into the past, except the past is still home to living families.",
