        "title": "Why Sail into the Unknown?",
        "reading": "In the 1400s, Europeans wanted spices, silk, and gold from Asia, but the land routes were long and dangerous. A sea route would make traders rich. New ships called caravels were faster and could sail closer to the wind. New tools like the compass and the astrolabe helped sailors navigate far from shore. Kings and queens paid for voyages hoping for wealth and new lands to claim. Sailors risked storms, hunger, and getting lost on an endless ocean. Some sought adventure, some sought riches, and some were forced to go. In 1492, Columbus sailed west for Spain, opening a new age of exploration that would change both Europe and the Americas forever. Navigation tools kept improving. The astrolabe measured stars, the compass found north, and later the sextant measured angles precisely. By the 1700s, sailors could finally measure longitude with accurate clocks. Each tool shrank the ocean a little more, until the whole world was mapped.",
