        "title": "Estevanico Crosses a Continent",
        "reading": "One remarkable explorer was Estevanico, a Black man from Morocco. In 1528 he sailed with the Narvaez expedition to Florida. The expedition fell apart: ships were lost, food ran out, and most of the men died. Estevanico was one of only four survivors. For eight years, he walked across North America, from Florida to Mexico. Along the way, he learned the languages of many Native tribes and became known as a healer. His knowledge of languages later made him the guide for an expedition into the American Southwest. Estevanico's journey was one of the greatest survival stories of the age of exploration, yet history books often leave him out. Estevanico was not the only African on early voyages. Free and enslaved Black sailors, navigators, and soldiers joined expeditions across the Americas. Their skills with languages, boats, and survival made many journeys possible. History is finally remembering these hidden explorers. Their names are being restored to the record, one voyage at a time, and the story of exploration grows truer with each one.",
