        "title": "From Chains to Webs",
        "reading": "A food chain shows one path of energy, but nature is messier than that. A rabbit eats grass, but it also eats clover. A hawk eats rabbits, but it also eats snakes and mice. When you connect many food chains together, you get a food web, a map of who eats whom in an ecosystem. Food webs are stronger than single chains. If one food disappears, an animal can eat something else. A fox that loses rabbits can hunt mice instead. But webs have limits. If too many links break at once, like in a drought that kills many plants, the whole web trembles. Healthy ecosystems have rich, tangled webs with many choices at every level. Food webs can be huge. Scientists have mapped webs with hundreds of species and thousands of connections. The most connected animals, called keystone species, hold the web together. Remove sea otters, and sea urchins explode, eating the kelp forest. Every web has its keystones.",
