        "title": "1492: Two Views",
        "reading": "In 1492, Christopher Columbus sailed west for Spain and reached islands in the Caribbean. From the European view, this was a great discovery that opened trade in gold, sugar, and new foods like potatoes and corn. From the Native view, it was the start of terrible troubles. European diseases spread quickly, and millions of Native people died because they had no resistance. Soldiers took land and forced Native people to work. Sailing with Columbus was Pedro Alonso Nino, a Black navigator from Spain who helped guide the ships. His story reminds us that Africans were part of these voyages from the very start. The same event can look very different depending on whose eyes you see it through, and good historians study every side. The debate over Columbus continues today. Some cities celebrate his voyage; others honor Indigenous Peoples' Day instead. Statues have been removed in many places. How we remember 1492 shows what we value now, not just what happened then. History is always being reexamined.",
