        "title": "The Fur Trade",
        "reading": "While Spain built missions in the south, France claimed the north. French traders wanted one thing above all: beaver fur. In Europe, beaver hats were the height of fashion, and traders grew rich. French voyageurs paddled canoes loaded with trade goods deep into Native lands. They traded metal tools, cloth, and beads for piles of soft pelts. Native trappers became skilled partners in the trade, and many French traders married into tribes, learning their languages and ways. The fur trade shaped Canada and the northern United States. But it also spread disease and sparked wars as tribes competed for hunting grounds. The beaver was hunted so heavily that it nearly disappeared in some regions. The fur trade finally faded when fashions changed and beavers grew scarce. Silk hats replaced beaver ones in Europe. By the late 1800s, the great canoe brigades were history. But the trade routes became the roads and towns of Canada. The paths of the voyageurs are still traveled today.",
