        "title": "Layers of the Rainforest",
        "reading": "A rainforest grows in layers, like a giant green apartment building. The emergent layer holds the tallest trees, rising above it all where eagles and monkeys travel. Below is the canopy, a thick roof of leaves where most rainforest life lives: sloths, toucans, and tree frogs. The understory is darker and cooler, home to jaguars and smaller plants waiting for a sunbeam. The forest floor is dim and damp, where decomposers break down fallen leaves and insects scurry. Each layer has its own animals, and many never leave their floor. Rain falls almost daily, feeding the layers from top to bottom in one of Earth's richest ecosystems. Rainforests make their own rain. Trees release water vapor that forms clouds, which rain back down. Cut too many trees and the cycle breaks, turning forest toward desert. Protecting rainforests protects the rain itself. The forest and the sky need each other. Protect the trees, and you protect the rain; lose the trees, and the sky itself changes.",
