        "title": "Adaptations: Nature's Solutions",
        "reading": "An adaptation is a body part or behavior that helps a living thing survive. A polar bear's thick fur and fat keep it warm in freezing cold. A cactus's waxy skin holds water in the desert. Birds that eat nectar have long, thin beaks that reach deep into flowers. Some adaptations are behaviors. Penguins huddle together to share warmth. Opossums play dead to fool predators. Adaptations develop over long stretches of time as living things with helpful traits survive and have babies. Every strange beak, foot, and feather tells a story of a problem nature solved. Adaptations can be extreme. The arctic ground squirrel drops its body temperature below freezing and survives. The bombardier beetle sprays boiling chemicals at attackers. The immortal jellyfish can revert to its young form. Nature's solutions are stranger than any invention. From freezing squirrels to boiling beetles, life finds a way in the strangest places. The wilder the challenge, the wilder the solution.",
