        "title": "Hiding in Plain Sight",
        "reading": "Camouflage is an adaptation that helps animals blend into their surroundings. A polar bear's white fur vanishes against snow. A stick insect looks exactly like a twig. Flatfish change their skin color to match the sandy bottom. Camouflage helps hunters sneak up on prey and helps prey hide from hunters. Some animals change with the seasons: the Arctic fox is brown in summer and white in winter. Chameleons shift colors to match leaves and bark. The better the match, the better the survival. In nature, the best hiders are often the ones you never see. Some animals combine camouflage and mimicry. The leaf-tailed gecko looks like a dead leaf and sways like one in the wind. The orchid mantis looks like a flower petal, luring the very insects it eats. Nature loves to mix its tricks, and the best deceivers use both. The best tricksters in nature never rely on just one disguise.",
