        "title": "Migration: Nature's Great Journeys",
        "reading": "Migration is the seasonal movement of animals from one place to another. Monarch butterflies fly up to 3,000 miles from Canada to Mexico. Arctic terns fly from the Arctic to Antarctica and back each year, the longest migration on Earth. Wildebeest cross African rivers full of crocodiles. Animals migrate to find food, escape cold, or reach safe places to have babies. They navigate by the sun, the stars, Earth's magnetic field, and even smell. Migration is dangerous: storms, predators, and hunger threaten travelers. Yet the journey is worth it, because the destination offers what home cannot in that season. Some migrations are vertical, not horizontal. Mountain goats climb to high meadows in summer and descend in winter. Whales dive deep to feed and surface to breathe. Even tiny plankton rise at night and sink by day. Up and down can be a migration too. Even the tiniest plankton migrate, rising each night and sinking each dawn.",
