        "title": "Your Amazing Skeleton",
        "reading": "Your skeleton is a frame of 206 bones that holds you up, protects you, and helps you move. Your skull protects your brain like a helmet. Your ribs guard your heart and lungs like a cage. Your spine is a stack of small bones that lets you bend and twist. Bones are alive: they grow, heal when broken, and make blood cells inside their soft marrow. Joints connect bones and let them move. Your knee is a hinge joint that bends one way, while your shoulder is a ball-and-socket joint that swings in circles. Without your skeleton, you would be a puddle on the floor. Bones tell stories long after death. Archaeologists learn a person's age, diet, and diseases from skeletons. Forensic scientists solve crimes with bone clues. Your skeleton is a diary written in calcium, recording everything from childhood growth to old injuries. Every skeleton is an autobiography written in calcium and time.",
