        "title": "Taxes Without a Voice",
        "reading": "After the French and Indian War, Britain needed money and decided the colonists should pay. Parliament passed the Stamp Act, taxing every piece of paper, from newspapers to playing cards. The Sugar Act taxed molasses. Colonists were furious, not just about the money but about the principle: they had no representatives in Parliament, so they said the taxes were unfair. Their rallying cry became no taxation without representation. Colonists boycotted British goods, refusing to buy tea, cloth, and paper. Merchants in London lost money and complained. Under pressure, Parliament agreed to repeal the Stamp Act, but it soon passed new taxes, and the cycle of protest began again. The protests worked more than once. Boycotts forced the repeal of the Stamp Act, the Townshend duties, and other taxes. Each victory taught colonists that united action beat British power. By 1773, they had a playbook for resistance: boycott, protest, and petition, in that order.",
