        "title": "Your Heart: A Tireless Pump",
        "reading": "Your heart is a fist-sized muscle that never takes a break. It beats about 100,000 times a day, pumping blood through 60,000 miles of vessels. Blood carries oxygen and nutrients to every cell and hauls away waste. The heart has four chambers: two on top receive blood, two on the bottom pump it out. One side sends blood to the lungs to pick up oxygen, the other side sends oxygen-rich blood to the body. Valves keep the blood flowing the right way, like one-way doors. You can feel your pulse in your wrist: each beat is your heart pushing blood through your body. Your blood vessels would stretch sixty thousand miles laid end to end. Red blood cells live about four months, and your body makes millions every second. Your heart never rests: it beats while you sleep, dream, and grow. It is the hardest-working muscle you own. It will beat about three billion times in your life without a single day off.",
