        "title": "Lungs and Breathing",
        "reading": "Every breath you take is your lungs at work. Air enters through your nose and mouth, travels down your windpipe, and splits into two tubes leading to your lungs. Inside, tiny air sacs called alveoli pass oxygen into your blood and collect carbon dioxide to breathe out. Your diaphragm, a muscle below your lungs, pulls down to draw air in and pushes up to push air out. You breathe about 20,000 times a day without thinking. Exercise makes you breathe faster because working muscles need more oxygen. Your lungs and heart are partners: lungs load the blood with oxygen, and the heart delivers it. You breathe about twenty thousand times a day. Each breath moves about a pint of air. Over a lifetime, that is enough air to fill hundreds of swimming pools. Your lungs work every second without a single conscious thought. Breathing is the quietest hard work your body does.",
