        "title": "Rocks and Minerals",
        "reading": "Rocks are made of minerals, which are natural solids with a crystal structure. Granite is a rock, but quartz and feldspar are the minerals inside it. You can identify minerals by their traits: color, hardness, and how they break. Talc is so soft you can scratch it with a fingernail. Diamond is so hard it scratches everything. Some minerals split into flat sheets, like mica, while others break into rough chunks. Geologists use these clues like detectives. The phone in your pocket holds dozens of minerals, from quartz in the glass to copper in the wires. Rocks record Earth's history. Sedimentary layers hold fossils that date the past. Igneous rocks can be dated by radioactive clocks. The oldest rocks on Earth are over four billion years old. Every stone is a page from a very long book. Geologists read rocks the way you read books, layer by layer. Every cliff face is a library of deep time.",
