        "title": "The Trap at Yorktown",
        "reading": "By 1781, the war had dragged on for six years. British General Cornwallis set up camp at Yorktown, Virginia, expecting supply ships from the sea. But French warships blocked the harbor while American and French armies surrounded the town by land. It was a perfect siege. For weeks, cannons pounded the British lines. Cornwallis tried to escape across the river, but a storm scattered his boats. With no food, no help, and no way out, he surrendered on October 19, 1781. Nearly 8,000 British soldiers laid down their arms while bands played. The victory at Yorktown ended the fighting and made American independence certain. Yorktown's battlefield is a national park today. Visitors can walk the siege lines and see the cannons' positions. Each October, reenactors replay the surrender. Standing where 8,000 soldiers laid down their arms, you can feel history under your feet. You can walk those siege lines today and feel history humming under your shoes.",
