        "title": "America's First Government",
        "reading": "After winning independence, the thirteen states needed a government. In 1781, they approved the Articles of Confederation, America's first constitution. It created a confederation, a loose union where each state kept most of its power. Congress could declare war and make treaties, but it could not tax the states or force them to obey laws. Each state printed its own money, and states argued over borders and trade. The government was too weak to pay soldiers, protect trade, or fix problems. It was like a team where every player is the captain and nobody follows the coach. The Articles' failure taught the founders what not to do. When they wrote the Constitution, they gave the new government power to tax, enforce laws, and regulate trade. Every weakness of the Articles became a strength of the Constitution. Failure was the best teacher the founders had. The Constitution's strengths were all learned from the Articles' failures.",
