        "title": "Petitions for Freedom",
        "reading": "Under the new state governments, free Black communities saw a chance to demand their rights. In Massachusetts, a group of Black men sent petitions asking for freedom and the right to vote, pointing out that the revolution had been fought for liberty. Prince Hall, a Black leader in Boston, organized petitions asking the state to end slavery. In other states, free Black people sued in courts for their freedom and sometimes won. These petitions showed that Black Americans took the revolution's promises seriously. They used the very ideas of liberty to demand a place in the new nation. Prince Hall went on to found the first Black Masonic lodge in America. His petitions kept coming, year after year. He never saw slavery end, but his organizing laid groundwork for the abolition movement. Persistence, he proved, is its own kind of power. He never saw slavery end, but every petition he wrote pushed the door a little wider.",
