        "title": "Digging into Soil Layers",
        "reading": "Soil is not just dirt. It has layers called horizons, like a layer cake. The top layer, topsoil, is dark and rich with decayed plants that feed new growth. Below it lies subsoil, lighter in color, holding minerals and clay. Deeper still is parent material, chunks of rock breaking down into soil. At the bottom sits bedrock, solid rock that has not broken down yet. Plant roots stretch through the top layers, drinking water and nutrients. Worms tunnel through topsoil, mixing and airing it. Each layer took thousands of years to form, and rich topsoil is one of nature's greatest treasures. It takes about five hundred years to make one inch of topsoil. That means the soil in a garden holds thousands of years of history. Erosion can strip it in a single storm. Protecting topsoil protects the work of millennia. Your garden's soil is older than the pyramids; treat it like the treasure it is.",
