        "title": "Measuring Soil Layers",
        "reading": "Scientists measure soil layers to learn how land was formed. They dig a pit and measure each horizon with a ruler. Topsoil might be 10 centimeters deep in a rich garden but only 2 centimeters in a desert. Subsoil stretches deeper, sometimes a full meter. They note the color, feel the texture, and test how water soaks through. Sandy soil drains fast, clay holds water tight, and loam, a mix of both, is best for gardens. Measuring turns a simple hole into a history book, with each layer telling part of the land's story. Gardeners read soil like a book. Dark, crumbly topsoil means healthy ground. Pale, hard soil needs compost and care. A simple squeeze test tells much: good soil holds together loosely, then crumbles. Your hands can read what laboratories confirm. Squeeze a handful: good soil holds its shape, then crumbles like cake. Your fingers can grade soil better than you might think.",
