        "title": "The Great Compromise",
        "reading": "In the hot summer of 1787, delegates from twelve states met in Philadelphia to fix the broken government. Big states wanted representation based on population. Small states wanted equal votes for each state. The argument grew heated until Roger Sherman proposed the Great Compromise: a Congress with two parts. The House of Representatives would be based on population, and the Senate would give each state two votes. Both sides gave up something, and both gained something. The compromise saved the convention. The delegates then wrote the Constitution, creating a stronger federal government with three branches to balance power. The Constitution is the oldest still-used written constitution in the world. It has been amended only twenty-seven times in over two hundred years. Its endurance comes from that summer of compromise in Philadelphia. Strong foundations, built by argument, last. No other nation still uses a constitution this old. Two centuries of arguments have only made its foundations stronger.",
