        "title": "Weathering and Erosion",
        "reading": "Weathering breaks rock apart, and erosion carries the pieces away. Water seeps into cracks, freezes, and expands, splitting rock like a wedge. Wind blasts sand against stone, slowly wearing it down. Plant roots push into tiny cracks and pry them wider. Once rock breaks into sediment, erosion moves it: rivers carry pebbles downstream, glaciers drag boulders for miles, and wind lifts dust across deserts. The Grand Canyon was carved by water erosion over millions of years. Weathering and erosion never stop, constantly reshaping Earth's surface grain by grain. Weathering creates soil, the foundation of farming. Without weathering breaking rock into minerals, plants could not grow. Every farm sits on weathered rock. The slow crumbling of mountains feeds the world. Every farm on Earth sits on weathered rock. Without weathering breaking stone into minerals, no plant could grow and no crop could feed us. Mountains crumble slowly so that fields can flourish. Destruction, given time, becomes dinner.",
