        "reading": "When the Constitution was written, many Americans worried it gave the government too much power. They demanded a Bill of Rights, a list of freedoms the government could never take away. In 1791, the first ten amendments were added. The First Amendment protects speech, religion, and the press. The Second protects the right to bear arms. Others protect against unfair searches, guarantee fair trials, and promise that powers not given to the government belong to the states and the people. The Bill of Rights was a promise: the government serves the people, not the other way around. The Bill of Rights keeps growing in meaning. Courts have applied it to new questions: free speech online, privacy in phones, fair trials for all. Each generation reinterprets these old words for new times. Ten amendments, written with quills, still govern the digital age. Ten amendments written with quills still guard your phone, your speech, and your freedom.",
