        "title": "What Causes Earthquakes?",
        "reading": "Earth's outer shell is cracked into giant pieces called tectonic plates that slowly move. When plates push against each other, pressure builds along cracks called faults. The rock bends until it snaps, releasing energy as an earthquake. The spot underground where it starts is the focus, and the point on the surface above is the epicenter. Most earthquakes happen along plate edges, like California's San Andreas Fault. Scientists measure quakes with seismographs, machines that draw wiggly lines showing the shaking. Small quakes happen daily, but big ones can topple buildings. The 1964 Alaska earthquake, magnitude 9.2, was America's largest. It lifted parts of the coast thirty feet and dropped others eight. Tsunamis crossed the Pacific. The quake taught scientists how giant subduction zones work, saving lives in future quakes. In 1964, Alaska shook with a magnitude 9.2 quake, America's largest ever recorded. The ground rose thirty feet in places and tsunamis raced across the Pacific. Scientists still use its lessons to save lives today.",
