        "title": "Was It a Good Deal?",
        "reading": "In 1803, President Thomas Jefferson bought a huge territory from France for $15 million. The Louisiana Purchase stretched from the Mississippi River to the Rocky Mountains and doubled the size of the United States overnight. The price was about four cents an acre, one of the greatest land deals in history. But was it a good deal? Supporters said it gave farmers land and secured the Mississippi River for trade. Critics said Jefferson had no right to buy land, since the Constitution never mentioned it. Native peoples who lived there were never asked at all. The purchase changed America forever, for better and for worse. Jefferson himself had doubts about the purchase. He believed in strict reading of the Constitution, which never mentioned buying land. But the deal was too good to refuse. He asked Congress to approve it quickly, setting a precedent for bold presidential action. Even Jefferson, the deal's champion, wondered if a president should have such power.",
