        "title": "Fossils: Clues to the Past",
        "reading": "Fossils are the preserved remains of ancient living things. Most form when an animal or plant is buried quickly in mud or sand. Over millions of years, minerals seep into bones or wood and turn them to stone. Some fossils are imprints, like a leaf pressed into mud that hardened. Others are trace fossils: footprints, burrows, or droppings that show how animals behaved. Fossils tell us what lived long ago, what they ate, and how Earth has changed. Without fossils, we would never know dinosaurs ruled for 165 million years. The La Brea Tar Pits in Los Angeles trap fossils in sticky asphalt. Mammoths, saber-toothed cats, and dire wolves sank there over thousands of years. Scientists still pull bones from the tar today. A city street hides an Ice Age graveyard. The tar still traps animals today, just as it did ten thousand years ago. Scientists watch the pits as both a window and a warning.",
