        "title": "Into the Unknown West",
        "reading": "After buying Louisiana, Jefferson needed to know what he had bought. He sent Meriwether Lewis and William Clark on an expedition to explore the new territory and find a water route to the Pacific. In 1804, their Corps of Discovery set out from St. Louis with about 40 men. They mapped rivers, met Native nations, and recorded hundreds of plants and animals new to science. Lewis wrote in his journal every night, even when exhausted. They crossed the Rocky Mountains, nearly starving, before reaching the Pacific Ocean in 1805. Their journey took over two years and filled in the map of America. Sacagawea, the Shoshone guide, was essential to the expedition. She found food, calmed tense meetings, and carried her baby the whole way. Her presence signaled peace to the tribes they met. Without her, the journey might have failed. Without her knowledge and courage, the expedition might never have reached the Pacific.",
