        "title": "The Water Cycle",
        "reading": "Water never leaves Earth. It just travels in a circle called the water cycle. The sun heats oceans and lakes, and water evaporates, rising as invisible vapor. High in the sky, the vapor cools and condenses into tiny droplets, forming clouds. When droplets grow heavy, they fall as precipitation: rain or snow. Some soaks into the ground, some flows in rivers back to the sea, and the cycle starts again. The water in your glass may once have been drunk by a dinosaur. Every drop is recycled endlessly. You can see the water cycle in a bag. Tape a plastic bag with a little water to a sunny window. Droplets form inside as water evaporates and condenses. It is a tiny ocean, sky, and rain in one bag. The whole cycle, on your windowsill. By afternoon your bag will be beaded with tiny droplets: a whole water cycle on a windowsill. Science is often that simple and that wonderful.",
