        "title": "Clouds: Reading the Sky",
        "reading": "Clouds are visible groups of tiny water droplets floating in the air. They form when warm, moist air rises and cools. Scientists name clouds by their looks. Cirrus clouds are thin and wispy, high up where it is cold. Cumulus clouds are puffy and white, the classic cartoon clouds. Stratus clouds are flat gray blankets that cover the sky. Towering cumulonimbus clouds bring thunderstorms. Watching clouds helps predict weather: wispy cirrus often means change is coming, while dark cumulonimbus means storms are near. Clouds have Latin names that describe them. Cirrus means curl, cumulus means heap, stratus means layer, nimbus means rain. Learning the roots lets you decode any cloud name. Science often hides its meanings in plain Latin. Cloud names hide their meanings in Latin: cirrus means curl, cumulus means heap, stratus means layer. Learn the roots and you can decode any cloud you see. Science often labels things plainly, if you know the language.",
