        "title": "Zones of the Ocean",
        "reading": "The ocean has layers called zones, each with its own light and life. The sunlight zone, from the surface to 660 feet, holds almost all ocean life: coral reefs, fish, and seaweed. The twilight zone, down to 3,300 feet, is dim and cold; animals here have big eyes. The midnight zone, down to 13,000 feet, is pitch black, and creatures make their own light. Below lies the abyss, near freezing with crushing pressure, and the trenches, the deepest places on Earth. Each zone is a different world, stacked one atop another in the deep blue sea. The deepest place on Earth is the Mariana Trench, nearly seven miles down. If Mount Everest sat in it, the peak would be a mile underwater. Strange fish live there in total darkness. We know more about the moon than about these depths. We have mapped the moon better than our own ocean floor. The deepest trench on Earth remains mostly unexplored.",
