        "title": "Tides: The Ocean Breathes",
        "reading": "Tides are the daily rise and fall of the ocean, caused mostly by the moon's gravity pulling on Earth's water. As Earth spins, the moon's pull creates a bulge of water that follows it around. Most coasts have two high tides and two low tides each day, about six hours apart. During full and new moons, the sun lines up with the moon and tides grow extra high and low, called spring tides. Tidal pools trap sea stars and crabs until the water returns. Tides shape coastlines and set the rhythm of shore life. Some places have almost no tides. In the Mediterranean, the tide barely rises a foot. In Canada's Bay of Fundy, it rises fifty feet. Shape of the coast decides everything. The same moon pulls, but each shore answers differently. The same moon pulls on every shore, yet each coast answers differently. Geography conducts the tide's orchestra.",
