        "title": "The Alamo Debate",
        "reading": "In the 1830s, American settlers in Texas, then part of Mexico, clashed with the Mexican government over slavery, taxes, and self-rule. In 1836, about 200 Texan defenders held the Alamo, an old mission in San Antonio, against thousands of Mexican soldiers led by Santa Anna. After thirteen days, the Mexicans stormed the fort and killed nearly all the defenders. The defeat became a rallying cry: Remember the Alamo. Weeks later, Texans won independence at San Jacinto. Was the Alamo a heroic last stand or a needless sacrifice? Historians still debate it. What is certain is that the battle shaped Texas identity forever. Texas was an independent republic for nine years before joining the United States in 1845. It kept its own debts, its own army, and its own pride. The Lone Star flag still flies over Texas today. Nine years of independence shaped a lasting identity. Nine years as a republic gave Texas a pride that still shows in its flag and its name.",
