        "title": "The Moon's Phases",
        "reading": "The moon seems to change shape, but it is always a round ball. What changes is how much of its sunlit side we see. The phases run in a cycle about 29 days long. It starts with the new moon, when the moon is dark. Then a crescent grows, the first quarter shows half, and the gibbous moon swells until the full moon shines round and bright. Then the light shrinks back through the same steps. The moon does not make its own light; it reflects the sun's. Watching the phases is watching sunlight and shadows play across a world 240,000 miles away. The moon is slowly drifting away from Earth, about an inch and a half per year. Long ago it was much closer and looked huge. In the far future, total solar eclipses will end. The moon's slow goodbye takes billions of years. The moon is leaving us at the speed your fingernails grow: slowly, but surely.",
