        "title": "Solids, Liquids, and Gases",
        "reading": "All matter comes in three common states. Solids, like ice and rock, have a fixed shape and volume because their particles are packed tight and barely move. Liquids, like water and juice, have a fixed volume but take the shape of their container because particles slide past each other. Gases, like air and steam, have no fixed shape or volume: their particles race around and fill any space. Heat changes states: ice melts to water, water boils to steam. Cooling reverses it. The same water can be all three states, showing that matter is never still. Plasma is a fourth state of matter, found in stars and lightning. It is gas so hot its atoms break apart. Neon signs glow with plasma. Most of the universe is plasma, though it is rare on Earth. Matter has more moods than three. Look at the sun and you are looking at plasma, the most common state of matter in the universe.",
