        "reading": "On January 1, 1863, President Lincoln issued the Emancipation Proclamation. It declared that all enslaved people in the rebelling states were free. It did not free everyone at once: it left slavery untouched in the loyal border states, and the Confederacy ignored it. But it changed the war's meaning forever. The fight was now openly about ending slavery, and Black men could join the Union army. Enslaved people heard the news and fled to Union lines by the thousands, freeing themselves. The proclamation was a bold step, though full freedom would need the 13th Amendment. The proclamation encouraged enslaved people to flee, and thousands did. They came to Union camps seeking freedom, and the army put many to work. These self-freed people forced the nation to face what freedom meant. They freed themselves before the law caught up. Enslaved people did not wait for paperwork; they ran to Union lines and freed themselves. The law followed their courage.",
