        "title": "How Heat Moves",
        "reading": "Heat moves in three ways, and two of them have special names. Conduction is heat moving through touch: a metal spoon in hot soup gets hot because heat travels up the metal. Convection is heat moving through flowing liquids or gases: warm air rises, cool air sinks, creating a loop. That is why the upstairs of a house is often warmer. Radiation, the third way, is heat traveling as waves, like sunlight warming your skin. Understanding heat movement helps engineers design everything from ovens to winter coats. Convection drives Earth's weather and geology. Hot air rises to make clouds; hot rock rises to move continents. The same physics shapes skies and mountains. Heat in motion is one of nature's master tools. Convection moves continents and brews weather with the same physics. Hot material rises, cool material sinks, and the cycle drives everything. Heat in motion is one of nature's master tools.",
