        "title": "Pulleys and Inclined Planes",
        "reading": "A pulley is a wheel with a rope that lifts loads with less effort. One pulley changes direction: pull down to lift up. More pulleys share the load, so each rope carries less weight. An inclined plane is a slanted surface, like a ramp. Pushing a heavy box up a gentle ramp takes less force than lifting it straight up. The longer the ramp, the easier the push, but the farther you must go. Ancient builders used ramps to raise stones for pyramids. Pulleys and ramps are simple machines that trade distance for force. Pulleys can even reverse direction, letting you pull down to lift up. Sailors use this to raise sails. Window blinds use tiny pulleys. Changing direction is sometimes as useful as multiplying force. Pulleys do more than multiply force; they can reverse direction entirely. Sailors pull down to raise sails upward, and window blinds run on tiny pulleys. Sometimes changing direction matters as much as adding strength.",
