        "title": "Friction: The Helpful Drag",
        "reading": "Friction is the force that resists sliding. It lets your shoes grip the floor and car brakes stop the car. Without friction, walking would be like skating on ice. But friction also causes wear: it heats engine parts and wears out shoe soles. Different surfaces have different friction: rubber grips, ice slips, sandpaper grabs. Engineers design tires, shoes, and brakes around friction's rules. It is the force that both helps and hinders, depending on what you need. Friction lets you write: the pencil grips the paper. Too little friction and skates slide; too much and engines overheat. Engineers tune friction like an instrument. Grip is a science. Friction lets your pencil grip the paper as you write. Too little friction and skates slide wild; too much and engines overheat. Engineers tune friction the way musicians tune instruments. Grip is a science. Engineers tune friction carefully, since the right grip makes machines work and the wrong grip wears them out.",
