        "reading": "Engineers solve problems in steps called the engineering design process. First, they ask: what is the problem? Then they imagine many possible solutions and plan the best one. Next, they create a prototype, a first version to test. Then they test it, watch what fails, and improve the design. This loop repeats until the solution works well. Every bridge, phone, and shoe went through these steps. Engineers learn as much from failures as from successes, because each failed test points to a better design. The design process loops: test results send you back to improve. Real engineers rarely get it right the first time. Each failure teaches the next version. Iteration is the secret of every invention. Real engineers almost never succeed on the first try. Each test reveals flaws, and each flaw improves the next version. Iteration, not genius, is the true mother of invention. Every prototype teaches its builders something new.",
